                <pb facs="0046" n="34" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg34"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation 3/456: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-23">January 23, 1942</date>.<lb/>[Received January 23—9:13
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>142. Following telegram has been sent to Rio, January 23, 5 p.m.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>For the Under Secretary. Saw Pedregal, Secretary of the Council of Ministers,
                        which accepts the latest Brazilian resolution but wishes to add the phrase
                        from original resolution about legislative approval. Pedregal now trying to
                        reach Rossetti with instructions to seek this addition and to support
                        resolution. This stand is predicated on alleged ground that here foreign
                        relations is an executive function and war is legislation and the resolution
                        is generally considered equal to a declaration of war.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
